00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.220
Hi everyone! Welcome to our videos about conservation.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:07.220
 Hi everyone! Welcome to our videos about conservation.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:08.220
Today I am going to be telling you about preventive conservation.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.220
Today I am going to be telling you about preventive conservation.

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:10.220
What is preventive conservation?

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:11.540
to be telling you about preventive conservation.
What is preventive conservation? It is what

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:17.750
we do in the museum to take care of the specimens
and objects that we have so that researchers

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:23.859
can use them in the future and so that all
of you can enjoy them when you visit the museum.\

00:00:23.859 --> 00:00:29.239
And what do we do when we do preventive conservation?
We take a look at the ten agents of deterioration.

00:00:29.239 --> 00:00:32.890
I am not going to tell you about the ten,
but I am going to tell you about a few. For

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:40.240
example, the light that shines on us all the
time or the sun and all that, can be very

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.200
problematic for collections, so we look at
visible light and ultraviolet light. When

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:51.390
we go to the beach or we are exposed to the
sun, our skin can become red after a while

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:57.920
of being exposed to the sun, but, the specimens
do not, obviously, have their skins become

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:03.270
red, and what can happen to them is that they
can start to bleach or they may start to have

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:06.340
color problems and they change these colors,
for example, on their feathers or the colors

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:10.710
in the textiles and all that. So, in preventive
conservation we use some things like this

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:13.820
device that I have here, called an Elsec,
and it looks like an old cell phone, but it

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:17.120
is not an old cell phone... What we do is
monitor, with these two monitors here, light,

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.140
for example, visible light. There it is telling
us that we have 621 lux, which is fine for

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:26.130
a lab, because it helps us see what we are
doing and working with the specimens. And

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.310
we can also monitor ultraviolet light. The
UV light is under 60, which is great. If we

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.400
have more than 75 it is a problem for the
specimens, so the values 

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.920
we have now mean that these specimens can
be here in the laboratory for a long time

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.010
and have no problems.\
Other things we have to take care of are temperature

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:42.530
and humidity, which we can also measure with
this fantastic little device. If I show you,

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.830
for example, the humidity, we are at less
than 55% and the temperature is around 71

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:48.390
Fahrenheit, so, these two numbers 
 are also good values 

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:50.950
for the specimens, and they allow us to be
with them in the laboratory for a good time.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.549
If we have very high temperatures and very
high humidity, what happens is that things

00:01:53.549 --> 00:01:57.380
begin to degrade very quickly, so in the museum
we always try to have temperatures and humidity

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.369
that are not only not high, but also without
fluctuations, because if there are fluctuations,

00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:12.070
that's when we start to have problems.\
And another thing that I am also going to

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:14.950
show you, is that we also have to clean the
specimens, so, when you have vacuum cleaners

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:26.109
in your house, you have more or less these
elements to use in the vacuum cleaners, but

00:02:26.109 --> 00:02:41.260
we in the museum have others that I am going
to show you, that they are very adorable.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:45.500
Look at the difference in size between the
brushes that we use in our homes and those

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:50.650
that we use in museum conservation. So, these
brushes are great because they are super delicate

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:58.099
and do not affect any of the specimens when
we are using the vacuum cleaner. I am not

00:02:58.099 --> 00:03:03.562
going to turn on the vacuum cleaner now because
it makes a lot of noise, but I am going to

00:03:03.562 --> 00:03:06.470
show you how we also clean the specimens with
other elements such as, for example, these

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:11.730
sponges, which are very similar to makeup
sponges that you can buy in pharmacies. And

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:16.230
here we have here a horse skull that was on
display in the Mammal Hall until very recently

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:25.230
in the museum and now is in the lab with me,
so I am going to show you... first, of course,

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:30.630
we have to put on gloves before touching any
specimens... I'm going to show you more or

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:41.680
less how we do to cleaning.
So here you see this area that has a lot of

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:48.000
dust. I'm going to come with this little sponge
and I'm going to start slowly. Look at the

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.000
difference after having passed the sponge
super slowly. Look at this, this is not very

00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:02.740
happy. But well, this is the process that
we have to do in conservation, which is done

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:10.720
very slowly, very calmly, so that none of
the specimens are damaged. So, here is a demonstration

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.700
of what we can do to take care of the specimens
in the museum, be it with the temperature,

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:19.009
with the humidity, with the light, with the
dust, and a lot of other things that we also

00:04:19.009 --> 00:04:23.070
do to take care of the specimens.
Do not miss the next videos that we are going

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:26.910
to have, which are cleaning and repairing
of feathers in taxidermy birds, and also of

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:28.630
bone. Thank you very much, see you later!  

